WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:12.800
Every human moment belongs to a calendar.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.760
Cosmic Universalism asks whether it can also be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.880
located within the much larger&nbsp;
coordinate of the Cosmic Breath.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.720
Here, May 12, 2025—the launch&nbsp;
date of the Cosmic Universalism&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.160
Statement—is entered into the CU-Time Converter.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.120
The result is preserved as an exact&nbsp;
fixed-point coordinate, with no digits hidden.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:39.040
The page also shows an Observable-Universe-aligned&nbsp;
coordinate and a derived age since the Big Bang.

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:44.960
These outputs translate one historical instant.&nbsp;
They do not, by themselves, assign a TOM phase.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.280
The structural diagram supplies&nbsp;
the next layer of interpretation.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:55.200
CU proposes a full Breath of&nbsp;
approximately 3.108 trillion years: 2.8&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.000
trillion years in expansion, followed&nbsp;
by 308 billion years in compression.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:04.640
The Observable Universe marker is&nbsp;
placed between B-TOM and C-TOM—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.840
structural proposition, not&nbsp;
an empirical measurement.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.640
At the boundary between measured time&nbsp;
and CU notation, distinctions matter.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.000
Cesium-133 defines a second through&nbsp;
a specified atomic transition.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:26.480
Planck time is a different derived natural&nbsp;
unit: approximately 5.39 × 10⁻⁴⁴ seconds.

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:29.600
CU does not identify a TOM with Planck time.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:33.520
A TOM remains sealed inside the&nbsp;
framework’s own structural ledger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.320
while empirical units appear as references.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.520
The Research Observatory&nbsp;
makes the boundary explicit.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:41.920
Source science, CU mathematics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:46.320
theoretical propositions, and open&nbsp;
questions are not interchangeable.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.200
Empirical findings may anchor a reference value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:52.800
while the larger Cosmic&nbsp;
Breath remains a CU proposal.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.600
Where evidence ends, the framework must say so.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:58.240
Where convention begins, it must be named.

00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.720
The linked Y-TOM-to-Z-TOM document&nbsp;
describes a bridge from 27 nanoseconds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.720
to one second and compares it&nbsp;
with the cesium-defined second.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:10.640
CU then interprets that passage&nbsp;
within its own notation.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:14.080
Beyond the bridge, a base-10&nbsp;
placeholder convention carries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.440
the deeper TOM sequence toward the Z-TOM reset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.440 --> 00:02:23.440
preserving the ledger from sub-ZTOM through&nbsp;
the complete 3.108-trillion-year cycle.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:30.640
Together, the converter, diagram, Observatory,&nbsp;
and source ledger locate one human event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:35.760
without erasing the boundary between&nbsp;
calculation, interpretation, and science.

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.080
May 12, 2025.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:55.200
A human moment placed within the Cosmic Breath.
